.TH E1000E 4 "2026-10-02" "Substrate Kernel" "Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
e1000e \- Intel 82574 and I217/I218/I219 Gigabit Ethernet driver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B device e1000e
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B e1000e
driver supports Intel's PCI Express Gigabit Ethernet controllers that
keep the 8254x register interface but need a different bring-up from the
parts
.B e1000
drives:
.TP
.B 82574L, 82574LA, 82583V
discrete controllers (8086:10D3, 10F6, 150C).  QEMU's
.B -device e1000e
is an 82574L.
.TP
.B I217, I218
the network MAC inside Lynx Point and Wildcat Point chipsets, with its
PHY on the chipset's MDIO bus.
.TP
.B I219
the same from Sunrise Point (6th-generation Core) onwards, through
I219-LM12/V12.
.PP
The interface is registered as
.BR eth0 .
One controller is supported; a second is reported and ignored.
.SS Data path
One receive and one transmit ring of legacy 16-byte descriptors, 256 and
128 entries, with 2 KiB buffers.  The Ethernet CRC is stripped on receive
and inserted on transmit, so a frame handed to the driver may be at most
1514 bytes, or 1518 with an 802.1Q tag.  Every supported part accepts
legacy descriptors while extended receive status is off, and the driver
turns it off in case a boot ROM left it on.
.PP
Transmit keeps one descriptor free, since the hardware reads a tail equal
to its head as an empty ring.  When the ring is full a sender that had
interrupts enabled waits up to 5 ms, outside the transmit lock, for a
slot; a sender in interrupt context gets
.B ENOBUFS
at once.  Frames are refused with
.B ENETDOWN
while there is no link.  Receive collects at most one ring's worth per
interrupt and re-raises the interrupt for the rest.
.PP
Multicast is received promiscuously; the IP layer filters by group
membership.  This keeps IPv6 neighbour discovery working without a
multicast table entry per address.
.PP
The station address is taken from Receive Address 0, which the hardware
loads from its NVM at reset.  The chipset parts keep that NVM in the
system's SPI flash rather than behind an EEPROM interface.  If the
reloaded address is invalid the one firmware left there is used; with
neither, the driver does not attach.  Receive Address entries beyond the
first are cleared.
.SS Bring-up
The driver points the ring registers at its own (empty) rings before
anything is enabled.  An I219 can hang in reset if descriptors were left
pending, for example after a PXE boot; the hardware reports this in PCI
configuration space, and the driver then pushes one dummy transmit
through its ring and, if that is not enough, cycles the receiver.  It
then stops new bus-master requests, waits for outstanding ones to drain,
and resets the MAC under the software semaphore in
.B EXTCNF_CTRL
that arbitrates shared resources with firmware.  On the 82574 it then
waits for the NVM reload and PHY configuration.
.PP
After reset the driver sets
.B CTRL_EXT.DRV_LOAD
so that manageability firmware (Intel AMT on vPro machines) hands the
port over, and applies the settings each family's documentation and
specification updates require: transmit arbitration, the Sunrise/Kaby
Point I219 transmit errata, packet-buffer ECC, snooped and strictly
ordered DMA, NFS filtering off, and on the 82574 its PCIe workarounds
with ASPM disabled.  With AMT pass-through active, ARP interception is
turned off so ARP requests reach the host.
.SS PHY
The PHY is reached through the MDIC register at MDIO address 1 (82574)
or 2 (chipset parts; pages from 768 at address 1), under the same
software semaphore.  A register read of 0 or all-ones, or an MDIC
completion for a different register, means the PHY did not answer.
.PP
On the chipset parts the PHY is first brought out of Ultra Low Power
mode (through the Management Engine if present, otherwise directly) and
out of forced SMBus mode.  If it still does not answer, the driver tries
forced SMBus mode, then a power cycle through the LANPHYPC pin when
firmware allows a PHY reset, then PCIe mode again.
.PP
Low Power Link Up and Gigabit-disable are cleared, 10/100 half and full
and 1000 full are advertised, and autonegotiation is restarted.  The I217
and I219 also get carrier sense on transmit, automatic downshift and
automatic MDI-X.
.SS Link and recovery
A kernel thread applies the chipset parts' speed-dependent settings at
every link change: inter-packet gap and PHY receive latency, PLL clock
gating, K1, the I219 pointer gap, the beacon duration, the I218 K1
workaround on low-power platforms, and Latency Tolerance Reporting.  The
interface's RUNNING flag follows the link.
.PP
The same thread resets and reprograms the controller (leaving the PHY and
link alone) when the transmit unit has made no progress for 3 seconds
with link up, or when the link drops with frames still queued.
.SS Interrupts
The driver tries MSI first, then the firmware's legacy interrupt line,
then legacy INTx routed through the I/O APIC by the conventional PCI
interrupt swizzle.  Each source is proven by raising a link-status-change
cause through the Interrupt Cause Set register: the vector must be quiet
before, deliver that cause, and be quiet again after.  One that fails is
removed and the next is tried.  If none can be proven, the first that
installs is kept with a warning.  On a shared line the handler acts only
when
.B ICR
reports that this device asserted it.  On the I219, legacy interrupts
keep the sideband clock their messages travel on ungated.
.PP
Interrupts are not throttled.  Measured under QEMU, throttling delayed
acknowledgement processing enough to stall bulk TCP uploads.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B sys/drivers/net/e1000e.c
The driver.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.B "e1000e: <part> <device-id> <MAC> msi|irq <n>"
Attached; followed by the link state.
.TP
.B "e1000e: MSI <n> verified"
The interrupt source that passed the self-test.
.TP
.B "e1000e: WARNING: using unverified ..."
No source passed the self-test; receive may not work.
.TP
.B "e1000e: descriptor flush required before reset (I219)"
The I219 reset-hang workaround ran.
.TP
.B "e1000e: PHY not responding on MDIO; left as found"
Every recovery step failed; the link may still come up if firmware
manages the PHY.
.TP
.B "e1000e: ...; resetting the controller"
The recovery thread reset the controller after a transmit hang or a link
loss with frames queued.
.SH BUGS
The older chipset PHYs \(em 82577 and 82578 (Ibex Peak) and 82579
(Cougar Point, Panther Point) \(em are not supported; they need
per-generation PHY workarounds.  When the NVM asks software to configure
the PHY after a reset, that configuration is not applied (it is logged).
No jumbo frames, checksum offload, or segmentation offload.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ifconfig (8)
.SH HISTORY
Written for Substrate in 2026, following Linux's
.B e1000e
and iPXE's
.B intel
drivers.  Tested under QEMU (82574L); the I217 and I219 support awaits
real hardware.
